{:edb/db-before-t 929 :edb/db-after-t 930 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534242 50 #inst "2026-10-11T18:08:48.327Z" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186203702 1071 "Which of the following integers has a multiplicative inverse modulo $25$?\n\n1. $7$\n2. $10$\n3. $12$" 13194139534242 17592186045417 false] [17592186203702 1071 "Which of the following integers has a multiplicative inverse modulo $25?$\n\n- $7$\n\n- $10$\n\n- $12$" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186203702 1074 "An integer $a$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $n$ if and only if $a$ and $n$ are coprime, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(a,n)=1\n$$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- $7$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $25$ since $\\textrm{gcd}(7,25)=1. \\:\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n- $10$ does *not* have a multiplicative inverse modulo $25$ since $\\textrm{gcd}(10,25)=5\\neq1. \\:\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n- $12$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $25$ since $\\textrm{gcd}(12,25)=1. \\:\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534242 17592186045417 false] [17592186203702 1074 "An integer $a$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $n$ if and only if $a$ and $n$ are coprime, i.e., $\\textrm{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- $7$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $25$ since $\\textrm{gcd}(7,25)=1. \\:\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\n- $10$ does *not* have a multiplicative inverse modulo $25$ since $\\textrm{gcd}(10,25)=5\\neq1. \\:\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n- $12$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $25$ since $\\textrm{gcd}(12,25)=1. \\:\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186203703 1071 "Use trial and error to compute the multiplicative inverse of $8$ modulo $15$." 13194139534242 17592186045417 false] [17592186203703 1071 "Use trial and error to compute the multiplicative inverse of $8$ modulo $15.$" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186203703 1074 "The multiplicative inverse of $8$ modulo $15$, denoted $8^{-1}$, is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n8 \\cdot x \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:15)\n$$\n\nNote that since\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(8,15) = 1\n$$\n\nan inverse element exists and is unique up to congruence modulo $15$.\n\nSince the modulus $n=15$ is small, we can find the inverse $x$ by computing exponents of $8$ (modulo $15$) until we get a result of $1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}8^{2} & ≡8 \\cdot 8≡64≡4 \\cdot 15 + 4≡4 & (mod15) \\\\ 8^{3} & ≡8 \\cdot 4≡32≡2 \\cdot 15 + 2≡2 & (mod15) \\\\ 8^{4} & ≡8 \\cdot 2≡16≡15 + 1≡1 & (mod15)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, $8 \\cdot 2 \\equiv 1 \\, (\\textrm{mod} \\:15)$. Therefore, the multiplicative inverse of $8$ modulo $15$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$." 13194139534242 17592186045417 false] [17592186203703 1074 "The multiplicative inverse of $8$ modulo $15,$ denoted $8^{-1},$ is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n8 \\cdot x \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:15).\n$$\n\nNote that since $\\text{gcd}(8,15) = 1,$ an inverse element exists and is unique up to congruence modulo $15.$\n\nSince the modulus $n=15$ is small, we can find the inverse $x$ by computing exponents of $8$ (modulo $15$) until we get a result of $1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{2} & ≡8\\cdot 8≡64≡4\\cdot 15+4≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ {8}^{3} & ≡8\\cdot 4≡32≡2\\cdot 15+2≡2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ {8}^{4} & ≡8\\cdot 2≡16≡15+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $8 \\cdot 2 \\equiv 1 \\, (\\textrm{mod} \\:15).$ Therefore, the multiplicative inverse of $8$ modulo $15$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}.$" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186203704 1071 "Use the extended Euclidean algorithm to compute the multiplicative inverse of $15$ modulo $86$." 13194139534242 17592186045417 false] [17592186203704 1071 "Use the extended Euclidean algorithm to compute the multiplicative inverse of $15$ modulo $86.$" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186203704 1074 "First of all, notice that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(15,86)=1\n$$\n\nSo, the multiplicative inverse of $15$ modulo $86$ exists.\n\nThe multiplicative inverse of $15$ modulo $86$ is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n15\\cdot x \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:86)\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $x$, we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n86 &= \\begin{vmatrix}15 \\cdot 5 & +\\end{vmatrix}11 \\\\\n↙\\begin{vmatrix}↙ \\\\ 15 &= \\end{vmatrix}11 \\cdot 1\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 4 \\\\ ↙ & ↙ \\\\ 11|= & 4 \\cdot 2\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}3 \\\\ ↙\\end{vmatrix}↙ \\\\\n4 &= \\begin{vmatrix}3 \\cdot 1 & +\\end{vmatrix}1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n11 &= 86 - 15 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n4 &= 15 - 11 \\\\\n3 &= 11 - 4 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n1 &= 4 - 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= 4 - 3 \\\\\n&= 4 - (11 - 4 \\cdot 2) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 3 - 11 \\\\\n&= (15 - 11) \\cdot 3 - 11 \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot 3 - 11 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot 3 - (86 - 15 \\cdot 5) \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot 23 - 86 \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot 23 + 86 \\cdot (-4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result modulo $86$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\cdot 23 + 86 \\cdot (-4) & ≡1 & (mod86) \\\\ 15 \\cdot 23 & ≡1 & (mod86)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $15\\cdot 23\\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:86)$, which means that\n\n$$\n15^{-1} \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: 86=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}23}\n$$" 13194139534242 17592186045417 false] [17592186203704 1074 "First of all, notice that $\\textrm{gcd}(15,86)=1.$ So, the multiplicative inverse of $15$ modulo $86$ exists.\n\nThe multiplicative inverse of $15$ modulo $86$ is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n15\\cdot x \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:86).\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $x,$ we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}86 & = & 15\\cdot 5 & + & 11 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 15 & = & 11\\cdot 1 & + & 4 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 11 & = & 4\\cdot 2 & + & 3 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 4 & = & 3\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11 & =86-15\\cdot 5 \\\\ 4 & =15-11 \\\\ 3 & =11-4\\cdot 2 \\\\ 1 & =4-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =4-3 \\\\  & =4-(11-4\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3-11 \\\\  & =(15-11)\\cdot 3-11 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 3-11\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 3-(86-15\\cdot 5)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 23-86\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 23+86\\cdot (-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result modulo $86,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15\\cdot 23+86\\cdot (-4) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,86) \\\\ 15\\cdot 23 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,86)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $15\\cdot 23\\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:86),$ which means that $15^{-1} \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: 86=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}23}.$" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186203912 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415620 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186203912 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415621 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186203913 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415618 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186203913 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415619 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186203914 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415616 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186203914 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415617 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186204110 1050 720.0 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186205241 1064 "The multiplicative inverse of an integer $a$ modulo $n$ is the unique number $x$ such that\n\n$$\na \\cdot x \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:n)\n$$\n\nThe word \"unique\" in the statement above means with respect to the inequality\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x < n\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\nx={\\color{red}{4}}\n$$\n\nis the multiplicative inverse of\n\n$$\na={\\color{blue}{6}}\n$$\n\nmodulo $23$ since\n$6 \\cdot 4\\begin{vmatrix}≡24 & (mod23) \\\\ ≡23 + 1 & (mod23) \\\\ ≡0 + 1 & (mod23) \\\\ ≡1 & (mod23)\\end{vmatrix}$.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We sometimes denote the multiplicative inverse of $a$ as $a^{-1}$. So, ${\\color{blue}{6}}^{-1} = {\\color{red}{4}}$ in this example.\n- Be careful not confuse the notation $a^{-1}$ with the reciprocal of $a$. In this context, both $a$ and $a^{-1}$ are *integers.*\n- The uniqueness of $a^{-1}$ is up to congruence modulo $n$. For example, since ${\\color{red}{27}}\\equiv {\\color{red}{4}}\\:(\\textrm{mod}23)$, we have\n$27 \\cdot 6\\begin{vmatrix}≡162 & (mod23) \\\\ ≡7 \\cdot 23 + 1 & (mod23) \\\\ ≡7 \\cdot 0 + 1 & (mod23) \\\\ ≡1 & (mod23)\\end{vmatrix}$.\n\nThere are circumstances when a multiplicative inverse does not exist. To this end, we have the following theorem:\n\n> *An integer $a$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $n$ if and only if $a$ and $n$ are coprime, i.e., $\\textrm{gcd}(a,n)=1$.*\n\nFor example:\n\n- We saw earlier that $6^{-1} = 4$ modulo $23$. Indeed, $\\textrm{gcd}(6,23) = 1$. Moreover, since $n=23$ is prime, *every* integer $1 \\leq x \\leq 22$ modulo $23$ has a multiplicative inverse. More on that in future lessons.\n- In contrast, the integer $a=2$ has no multiplicative inverse modulo $14$ since $\\textrm{gcd}(2,14) = 2\\neq 1$. Intuitively, multiplying $a = 2$ by any integer $x$ modulo $14$ gives an even number, and taking the result modulo $14$ (also even) will never give us $1$ (an odd integer)." 13194139534242 17592186045417 false] [17592186205241 1064 "The multiplicative inverse of an integer $a$ modulo $n$ is the unique number $x$ such that\n\n$$\na \\cdot x \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:n).\n$$\n\nThe word \"unique\" in the statement above means with respect to the inequality $0 \\leq x < n.$ \n\nFor example, $x={\\color{red}{4}}$ is the multiplicative inverse of $a={\\color{blue}{6}}$ modulo $23$ since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot 4 & ≡24 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,23) \\\\  & ≡23+1 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,23) \\\\  & ≡0+1 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,23) \\\\  & ≡1 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,23) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following: \n\n- We sometimes denote the multiplicative inverse of $a$ as $a^{-1}.$ So, ${\\color{blue}{6}}^{-1} = {\\color{red}{4}}$ in this example.\n\n- Be careful not confuse the notation $a^{-1}$ with the reciprocal of $a.$ In this context, both $a$ and $a^{-1}$ are *integers.*\n\n- The uniqueness of $a^{-1}$ is up to congruence modulo $n.$ For example, since ${\\color{red}{27}}\\equiv {\\color{red}{4}}\\:(\\textrm{mod }23),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27\\cdot 6 & ≡162 & (\\text{mod}\\,23) \\\\  & ≡7\\cdot 23+1 & (\\text{mod}\\,23) \\\\  & ≡7\\cdot 0+1 & (\\text{mod}\\,23) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,23) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are circumstances when a multiplicative inverse does not exist. To this end, we have the following theorem:\n\n*An integer $a$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $n$ if and only if $a$ and $n$ are coprime, i.e., $\\textrm{gcd}(a,n)=1.$*\n\nFor example:\n\n- We saw earlier that $6^{-1} = 4$ modulo $23.$ Indeed, $\\textrm{gcd}(6,23) = 1.$ Moreover, since $n=23$ is prime, *every* integer $1 \\leq x \\leq 22$ modulo $23$ has a multiplicative inverse. More on that in future lessons.\n\n- In contrast, the integer $a=2$ has no multiplicative inverse modulo $14$ since $\\textrm{gcd}(2,14) = 2\\neq 1.$ Intuitively, multiplying $a = 2$ by any integer $x$ modulo $14$ gives an even number, and taking the result modulo $14$ (also even) will never give us $1$ (an odd integer)." 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186205249 1064 "Now, suppose we want to compute the multiplicative inverse of $23$ modulo $79$.\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(23,79)=1\n$$\n\nthe inverse exists. However, the modulus $n=79$ is quite big, so finding the inverse by trial and error is inefficient. So, what do we do?\n\nTo answer this, recall that Bézout's identity states that there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that\n\n$$\n23u + 79v = \\textrm{gcd}(23,79)\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(23,79)=1\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n23u + 79v = 1\n$$\n\nWe can find the integers $u$ and $v$ using the extended Euclidian algorithm.\n\nApplying the Euclidian algorithm (we'll skip over the details for now), we find that\n\n$$\nu=-24, v = 7\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n23 \\cdot (-24) + 7 \\cdot 79 = 1\n$$\n\nTo find the multiplicative inverse of $23$, the key is to write this result modulo $79$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}23 \\cdot (-24) + 7 \\cdot 79 & ≡1 & (mod79) \\\\ 23 \\cdot (-24) + 7 \\cdot 0 & ≡1 & (mod79) \\\\ 23 \\cdot (-24) & ≡1 & (mod79) \\\\ 23 \\cdot (0 - 24) & ≡1 & (mod79) \\\\ 23 \\cdot (79 - 24) & ≡1 & (mod79) \\\\ 23 \\cdot 55 & ≡1 & (mod79)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $23 \\cdot 55 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:79)$. Therefore, $55$ is the multiplicative inverse of $23$ (modulo $79$).\n\nFinally, we can express this result using the residue function as follows:\n\n$$\n23^{-1} \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: 79 = 55\n$$" 13194139534242 17592186045417 false] [17592186205249 1064 "Now, suppose we want to compute the multiplicative inverse of $23$ modulo $79.$ \n\nSince $\\textrm{gcd}(23,79)=1,$ the inverse exists. However, the modulus $n=79$ is quite big, so finding the inverse by trial and error is inefficient. So, what do we do?\n\nTo answer this, recall that Bézout's identity states that there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that\n\n$$\n23u + 79v = \\textrm{gcd}(23,79).\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\textrm{gcd}(23,79)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n23u + 79v = 1.\n$$\n\nWe can find the integers $u$ and $v$ using the extended Euclidian algorithm.  \n\nApplying the Euclidian algorithm (we'll skip over the details for now), we find that $u=-24, v = 7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n23 \\cdot (-24) + 7 \\cdot 79 = 1.\n$$\n\nTo find the multiplicative inverse of $23,$ the key is to write this result modulo $79,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}23\\cdot (-24)+7\\cdot 79 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,79) \\\\ 23\\cdot (-24)+7\\cdot 0 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,79) \\\\ 23\\cdot (-24) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,79) \\\\ 23\\cdot (0-24) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,79) \\\\ 23\\cdot (79-24) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,79) \\\\ 23\\cdot 55 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,79)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $23 \\cdot 55 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:79).$ Therefore, $55$ is the multiplicative inverse of $23$ (modulo $79$).\n\nFinally, we can express this result using the residue function as follows:\n\n$$\n23^{-1} \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: 79 = 55\n$$" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186205255 1064 "Suppose we wish to calculate the multiplicative inverse of $3$ modulo $14$. In other words, we want to find a number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n3 \\cdot x \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:14)\n$$\n\nFirstly, we note that since\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(3,14) = 1\n$$\n\na unique multiplicative inverse exists.\n\nSince the modulus $n=14$ is fairly small, one way to efficiently compute the inverse is to use trial and error. To do this, we compute powers of $3$ and take the residue modulo $14$ until we get a result of $1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3^{2} & ≡3 \\cdot 3≡9 & (mod14) \\\\ 3^{3} & ≡3 \\cdot 3^{2}≡3 \\cdot 9≡27≡1 \\cdot 14 + 13≡13 & (mod14) \\\\ 3^{4} & ≡3 \\cdot 3^{3}≡3 \\cdot 13≡39≡2 \\cdot 14 + 11≡11 & (mod14) \\\\ 3^{5} & ≡3 \\cdot 3^{4}≡3 \\cdot 11≡33≡2 \\cdot 14 + 5≡5 & (mod14) \\\\ 3^{6} & ≡3 \\cdot 3^{5}≡3 \\cdot 5≡15≡1 \\cdot 14 + 1≡1 & (mod14)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAccording to the last line, we have\n$3 \\cdot {\\color{magenta}{5}} \\equiv 1\\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\:14)$.\n\nTherefore, the multiplicative inverse of $3$ modulo $14$ is ${\\color{magenta}{5}}$, and we can write\n\n$$\n3^{-1} = {\\color{magenta}{5}}\\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\:14)\n$$\n\n**Note:** We could get the same result by substituting\n\n$$\nx=1,2,\\ldots, 13\n$$\n\ninto\n$3 \\cdot x \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:14)$\nuntil we get the correct congruence relation. However, the exponentiation method described above typically allows us to find the solution with fewer trials since the multiplicative inverse of $a$ (if exists) must appear among the powers of $a$ modulo $n$." 13194139534242 17592186045417 false] [17592186205255 1064 "Suppose we wish to calculate the multiplicative inverse of $3$ modulo $14.$ In other words, we want to find a number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n3 \\cdot x \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:14).\n$$\n\nFirstly, we note that since $\\textrm{gcd}(3,14) = 1,$ a unique multiplicative inverse exists. \n\nSince the modulus $n=14$ is fairly small, one way to efficiently compute the inverse is to use trial and error. To do this, we compute powers of $3$ and take the residue modulo $14$ until we get a result of $1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2} & ≡3\\cdot 3≡9 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\ {3}^{3} & ≡3\\cdot {3}^{2}≡3\\cdot 9≡27≡1\\cdot 14+13≡13 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\ {3}^{4} & ≡3\\cdot {3}^{3}≡3\\cdot 13≡39≡2\\cdot 14+11≡11 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\ {3}^{5} & ≡3\\cdot {3}^{4}≡3\\cdot 11≡33≡2\\cdot 14+5≡5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\ {3}^{6} & ≡3\\cdot {3}^{5}≡3\\cdot 5≡15≡1\\cdot 14+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,14)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the last line, we have\n\n$$\n3 \\cdot {\\color{magenta}{5}}   \\equiv 1\\qquad  (\\textrm{mod}\\:14).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the multiplicative inverse of $3$ modulo $14$ is ${\\color{magenta}{5}},$ and we can write \n\n$$\n3^{-1} = {\\color{magenta}{5}}\\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\:14).\n$$\n\n**Note:** We could get the same result by substituting $x=1,2,\\ldots, 13$ into \n\n$$\n3 \\cdot x \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\:14)\n$$\n\nuntil we get the correct congruence relation. However, the exponentiation method described above typically allows us to find the solution with fewer trials since the multiplicative inverse of $a$ (if exists) must appear among the powers of $a$ modulo $n.$" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415584 1080 "1" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415584 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415584 1083 #uuid "104e7c32-f9c9-51bc-ae0f-4c410215746f" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415585 1080 "1" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415585 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415585 1083 #uuid "160d5f45-9189-5260-9c2f-7a57f82aad3f" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415586 1080 "I and III only" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415586 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415586 1083 #uuid "161735ec-56d4-5072-8a75-665310f43f19" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415587 1080 "II and III only" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415587 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415587 1083 #uuid "4c5e7ff7-af02-5c83-9cc7-4a20cdd2b462" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415588 1080 "-17" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415588 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415588 1083 #uuid "5539c33b-1dce-5393-93a1-4064a290b70d" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415589 1080 "33" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415589 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415589 1083 #uuid "5b74eb17-4d64-5719-a0ec-bfc1de3209b3" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415590 1080 "33" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415590 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415590 1083 #uuid "60a83021-eb0b-5bd6-adbb-9205cb87d642" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415591 1080 "-13" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415591 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415591 1083 #uuid "67ddaadf-b1b7-5b7d-8