ward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}258 & = & 55\\cdot 4 & + & 38 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 55 & = & 38\\cdot 1 & + & 17 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 38 & = & 17\\cdot 2 & + & 4 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 17 & = & 4\\cdot 4 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}38 & =258-55\\cdot 4 \\\\ 17 & =55-38 \\\\ 4 & =38-17\\cdot 2 \\\\ 1 & =17-4\\cdot 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =17-4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =17-(38-17\\cdot 2)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =17\\cdot 9-38\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =(55-38)\\cdot 9-38\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =55\\cdot 9-38\\cdot 13 \\\\  & =55\\cdot 9-(258-55\\cdot 4)\\cdot 13 \\\\  & =55\\cdot 61+258\\cdot (-13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result modulo $258,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}55\\cdot 61+258\\cdot (-13) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,258) \\\\ 55\\cdot 61 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,258)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $55\\cdot 61≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,258),$ which means that ${55}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,258=61.$" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415617 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415606 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415617 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415607 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415617 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415611 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415617 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415615 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415617 1069 "q-213532" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415617 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415617 1071 "Fill in the blanks to make the following reasoning true.\n\nSince $\\text{gcd}(50,97)=$\n{{field-1}}, \nby the extended Euclidean algorithm, we have \n\n| $1\\,=\\,50\\,\\cdot ($\n{{field-2}}\n$)+\\,97\\,\\cdot ($\n{{field-3}}\n$).$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, ${50}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,97=$\n{{field-4}} (expressed as the smallest nonnegative residue)." 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415617 1072 #uuid "3e2bafbf-e373-5f9a-9cd6-8fbc664e6471" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415617 1074 "First of all, notice that $\\text{gcd}(50,97)=1.$ So, the multiplicative inverse of $50$ modulo $97$ exists.\n\nThe multiplicative inverse of $50$ modulo $97$ is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n50\\cdot x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,97).\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $x,$ we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}97 & = & 50\\cdot 1 & + & 47 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 50 & = & 47\\cdot 1 & + & 3 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 47 & = & 3\\cdot 15 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 3 & = & 2\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}47 & =97-50 \\\\ 3 & =50-47 \\\\ 2 & =47-3\\cdot 15 \\\\ 1 & =3-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =3-2 \\\\  & =3-(47-3\\cdot 15) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 16-47 \\\\  & =(50-47)\\cdot 16-47 \\\\  & =50\\cdot 16-47\\cdot 17 \\\\  & =50\\cdot 16-(97-50)\\cdot 17 \\\\  & =50\\cdot 33-97\\cdot 17 \\\\  & =50\\cdot 33+97\\cdot (-17)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result modulo $97,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}50\\cdot 33+97\\cdot (-17) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,97) \\\\ 50\\cdot 33 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,97)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $50\\cdot 33≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,97),$ which means that ${50}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,97=33.$" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415618 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415605 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415618 1069 "q-213441" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415618 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415618 1071 "Use trial and error to compute the following residue.\n\n${3}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,11=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415618 1072 #uuid "4915f9fb-f03b-5b7e-8a32-e3e0683d3cad" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415618 1074 "The multiplicative inverse of $3$ modulo $11,$ denoted ${3}^{-1},$ is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n3\\cdot x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11).\n$$\n\nNote that since $\\text{gcd}(3,11)=1,$ an inverse element exists and is unique up to congruence modulo $11.$\n\nSince the modulus $n=11$ is small, we can find the inverse $x$ by computing exponents of $3$ (modulo $11$) until we get a result of $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2} & ≡3\\cdot 3≡9 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {3}^{3} & ≡3\\cdot 9≡27≡2\\cdot 11+5≡5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {3}^{4} & ≡3\\cdot 5≡15≡11+4≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {3}^{5} & ≡3\\cdot 4≡12≡11+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $3\\cdot 4≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11).$ Therefore, the multiplicative inverse of $3$ modulo $11$ is $4.$" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415619 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415614 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415619 1069 "q-213414" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415619 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415619 1071 "Use trial and error to compute the following residue.\n\n${3}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,5=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415619 1072 #uuid "675f6fdb-8d6a-5e1a-8484-30f4c73cb80a" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415619 1074 "The multiplicative inverse of $3$ modulo $5,$ denoted ${3}^{-1},$ is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n3\\cdot x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5).\n$$\n\nNote that since $\\text{gcd}(3,5)=1,$ an inverse element exists and is unique up to congruence modulo $5.$\n\nSince the modulus $n=5$ is small, we can find the inverse $x$ by computing exponents of $3$ (modulo $5$) until we get a result of $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2} & ≡3\\cdot 3≡9≡5+4≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ {3}^{3} & ≡3\\cdot 4≡12≡2\\cdot 5+2≡2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ {3}^{4} & ≡3\\cdot 2≡6≡5+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $3\\cdot 2≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5).$ Therefore, the multiplicative inverse of $3$ modulo $5$ is $2.$" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415620 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415612 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415620 1069 "q-213405" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415620 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415620 1071 "Which of the following integers has a multiplicative inverse modulo $36?$\n\n- $5$\n- $13$\n- $24$" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415620 1072 #uuid "6d34f2bd-1406-50c4-9338-867f1ecca34e" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415620 1074 "An integer $a$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $n$ if and only if $a$ and $n$ are coprime, i.e., $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- $5$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $36$ since $\\text{gcd}(5,36)=1.\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $13$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $36$ since $\\text{gcd}(13,36)=1.\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $24$ does *not* have a multiplicative inverse modulo $36$ since $\\text{gcd}(24,36)=12\\ne 1.\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415621 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415610 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415621 1069 "q-213407" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415621 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415621 1071 "Which of the following integers has a multiplicative inverse modulo $27?$\n\n- $11$\n- $16$\n- $23$" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415621 1072 #uuid "af0eb712-6f36-542d-b37c-e07c5fedf4a8" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true] [17592186415621 1074 "An integer $a$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $n$ if and only if $a$ and $n$ are coprime, i.e., $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- $11$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $27$ since $\\text{gcd}(11,27)=1.\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $16$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $27$ since $\\text{gcd}(16,27)=1.\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $23$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $27$ since $\\text{gcd}(23,27)=1.\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139534242 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-104e7c32-f9c9-51bc-ae0f-4c410215746f" 17592186415584 "answer-160d5f45-9189-5260-9c2f-7a57f82aad3f" 17592186415585 "answer-161735ec-56d4-5072-8a75-665310f43f19" 17592186415586 "answer-4c5e7ff7-af02-5c83-9cc7-4a20cdd2b462" 17592186415587 "answer-5539c33b-1dce-5393-93a1-4064a290b70d" 17592186415588 "answer-5b74eb17-4d64-5719-a0ec-bfc1de3209b3" 17592186415589 "answer-60a83021-eb0b-5bd6-adbb-9205cb87d642" 17592186415590 "answer-67ddaadf-b1b7-5b7d-8385-f1f708518b6d" 17592186415591 "answer-7aad8420-3c48-5474-98cb-d9e00f367a26" 17592186415592 "answer-7dd57479-5967-51e1-bdfa-54df7817cc48" 17592186415593 "answer-92df733c-9e8a-5b23-a107-168e6eed32b0" 17592186415594 "answer-a41fc355-034f-5ded-bdfb-ebfb582a1bda" 17592186415595 "answer-b03c9ec0-7f75-5d97-abbd-94b2869f47ad" 17592186415596 "answer-b0c4fbb0-762c-5b67-afd1-1fb5e5110f8b" 17592186415597 "answer-b51e64bf-376c-52bb-ac74-2778610f3e9f" 17592186415598 "answer-c5b0044e-5976-528d-9010-0418652be505" 17592186415599 "answer-d07425cc-ed1f-5b0a-8751-4e3973904462" 17592186415600 "answer-d66363e6-df16-5391-b2d0-6a090267e3a9" 17592186415601 "answer-f2f7e142-5fde-519c-99ea-9b33ca10f2d7" 17592186415602 "answer-f3f743a1-b3bd-5c44-85bc-086b14acdf8d" 17592186415603 "answer-field-1a797595-f7b3-5fbe-8e81-bec203cecb7b" 17592186415604 "answer-field-25bf507a-8609-584a-8895-2544c9926a42" 17592186415605 "answer-field-2a0ab631-1b27-5aa9-aef1-a92dc676b3f2" 17592186415606 "answer-field-89c3c4d5-f51f-5d05-ab6e-1c068b6cd4f9" 17592186415607 "answer-field-93f5dfb3-b0bd-54d4-9f0c-3a23cd8eb0e9" 17592186415608 "answer-field-a57d0469-969b-5d66-a222-054cf955e5d0" 17592186415609 "answer-field-a9802078-fa7f-52a5-a1e5-14125231ee58" 17592186415610 "answer-field-af0eb2c2-9bb3-53f5-8685-d1b8